1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,870
Imagine you have a company.

2
00:00:02,170 --> 00:00:05,440
Let's call it Northway,
a small software company.

3
00:00:05,806 --> 00:00:10,246
Every product it launched started the same
way: buy the domain first.

4
00:00:10,546 --> 00:00:12,316
Then one for each country.

5
00:00:12,616 --> 00:00:15,826
A few misspellings,
so nobody else grabs your visitors.

6
00:00:16,096 --> 00:00:18,496
And the old name, from before the rebrand.

7
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:22,727
Today, Northway owns over two hundred
domains.

8
00:00:23,027 --> 00:00:26,597
Whatever your business is,
your list probably grew the same way.

9
00:00:27,008 --> 00:00:30,188
Now you're bringing all your products
together on one website,

10
00:00:30,248 --> 00:00:32,948
so customers find everything in one place.

11
00:00:33,188 --> 00:00:37,418
That means every one of those two hundred
domains needs a new destination.

12
00:00:38,143 --> 00:00:41,693
But the real question is,
how do you move hundreds of domains at

13
00:00:41,743 --> 00:00:45,613
once, with one spreadsheet,
without losing the people who use them?

14
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:50,640
Each domain renews every year,
whether anyone uses it or not.

15
00:00:51,027 --> 00:00:52,705
Most of them forward somewhere.

16
00:00:53,035 --> 00:00:56,015
Set up years ago,
in whichever registrar account they were

17
00:00:56,065 --> 00:00:59,185
bought in. For Northway,
that's three accounts.

18
00:00:59,614 --> 00:01:01,144
Nobody has the full list.

19
00:01:01,414 --> 00:01:05,564
And nobody knows which ones people still
type, like the name of a product you

20
00:01:05,614 --> 00:01:06,994
retired years ago.

21
00:01:07,652 --> 00:01:11,132
One evening, a customer types that old
product domain.

22
00:01:11,462 --> 00:01:13,182
It forwards to Northway's site.

23
00:01:13,232 --> 00:01:16,922
Someone checked that once,
years ago. It worked.

24
00:01:17,256 --> 00:01:20,796
But the customer's browser asks for the
secure version first,

25
00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:22,206
the one with the padlock.

26
00:01:22,446 --> 00:01:25,956
Some registrars' forwarding includes one.
Others don't.

27
00:01:26,646 --> 00:01:28,716
So the customer sees a warning page.

28
00:01:28,956 --> 00:01:32,406
To them, it looks like Northway is broken.
They leave.

29
00:01:33,263 --> 00:01:34,752
Northway never finds out.

30
00:01:34,992 --> 00:01:38,412
Their own check worked only because they
typed the plain address.

31
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:41,697
The fix wasn't fewer domains.

32
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:45,837
Every domain needed two things:
a door that opens properly,

33
00:01:45,897 --> 00:01:49,407
padlock included, and a way to hear when
someone knocks.

34
00:01:50,508 --> 00:01:51,768
One honest note.

35
00:01:52,098 --> 00:01:55,618
If you have a handful of domains,
and your registrar's forwarding shows the

36
00:01:55,668 --> 00:01:57,198
padlock, you're done.

37
00:01:57,498 --> 00:01:59,748
Check one in our free Redirect Checker.

38
00:02:00,202 --> 00:02:04,642
But two hundred domains,
in three accounts, one form at a time?

39
00:02:04,912 --> 00:02:06,562
That's how domains get missed.

40
00:02:06,802 --> 00:02:11,782
So instead, you write down once where
every domain should go. In a spreadsheet.

41
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:14,420
Each row is one domain.

42
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,400
Next to it, where it should go.

43
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:18,980
The old product name to its new page.

44
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:21,290
Each country to its own pages.

45
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:23,450
The misspellings to the homepage.

46
00:02:23,878 --> 00:02:25,587
Then the kind of redirect.

47
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:28,227
A 301,
the permanent kind,

48
00:02:28,407 --> 00:02:30,417
because these moves are for good.

49
00:02:30,873 --> 00:02:36,123
And a tag, a short label for the group.
Product. Country. Typo.

50
00:02:36,483 --> 00:02:37,533
It pays off later.

51
00:02:37,967 --> 00:02:42,376
That one file is the whole plan,
and anyone on the team can check it.

52
00:02:43,103 --> 00:02:46,343
In RedirHub,
it goes in through Links, Create,

53
00:02:46,553 --> 00:02:47,903
Import CSV.

54
00:02:48,173 --> 00:02:51,893
That's the plain spreadsheet file Excel or
Google Sheets can save.

55
00:02:52,301 --> 00:02:55,451
Before anything is saved,
it shows what will happen.

56
00:02:55,871 --> 00:02:59,351
Every domain it adds,
and any row that needs a second look.

57
00:02:59,707 --> 00:03:02,537
A destination missing its https?

58
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:04,357
One click fixes all of them.

59
00:03:04,597 --> 00:03:08,197
And each domain's www version comes along too.

60
00:03:08,819 --> 00:03:13,379
One thing a spreadsheet can't do is tell
the internet where each domain lives.

61
00:03:13,649 --> 00:03:17,459
That's DNS, the setting that points a
domain somewhere.

62
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:20,350
On Cloudflare,
it can connect automatically.

63
00:03:20,710 --> 00:03:23,080
Elsewhere, you add the records it shows
you.

64
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:24,790
In batches, if you like.

65
00:03:25,128 --> 00:03:29,838
And as each domain connects,
its padlock is set up, and renewed for it.

66
00:03:30,923 --> 00:03:34,761
Then, for the first time,
Northway could hear the knocks.

67
00:03:35,061 --> 00:03:37,401
Visits, domain by domain.

68
00:03:37,868 --> 00:03:41,528
A few weeks in,
one stood out. A misspelling.

69
00:03:41,888 --> 00:03:43,778
Northway without the H.

70
00:03:44,018 --> 00:03:45,848
Sixty-three visits already.

71
00:03:46,229 --> 00:03:48,839
It had nearly been dropped at the last
renewal.

72
00:03:49,688 --> 00:03:52,658
Those were customers,
typing the name a little wrong.

73
00:03:52,988 --> 00:03:55,808
Now they land on the homepage,
padlock and all.

74
00:03:56,606 --> 00:03:58,796
The domains that stayed quiet all season?

75
00:03:59,126 --> 00:04:02,306
At renewal time,
Northway knows where to look first.

76
00:04:02,623 --> 00:04:04,003
And the tags pay off.

77
00:04:04,273 --> 00:04:05,443
Retiring a product?

78
00:04:05,623 --> 00:04:11,896
Filter by its tag. Change it once. So. One
list. One import.

79
00:04:12,166 --> 00:04:13,216
Point the domains.

80
00:04:13,486 --> 00:04:16,216
That's how to move hundreds of domains at
once.

81
00:04:17,027 --> 00:04:19,277
Northway isn't a real company, by the way.

82
00:04:19,637 --> 00:04:22,937
But this story started with "imagine" for
a reason.

83
00:04:23,388 --> 00:04:24,678
Picture your own list.

84
00:04:24,978 --> 00:04:27,198
Which domain would a customer type
tonight?

85
00:04:27,468 --> 00:04:29,388
And would the door open for them?

86
00:04:30,569 --> 00:04:32,249
If this helped, subscribe.

87
00:04:32,429 --> 00:04:33,839
Links are in the description.

88
00:04:34,109 --> 00:04:37,389
And if you're new to redirects,
our earlier videos are right here on

89
00:04:37,439 --> 00:04:38,519
screen.

